{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"write-letter-of-explanation-for-visa","kind":"prompt","title":"Write a letter of explanation for a visa file","description":"Writes a short, factual letter of explanation for an immigration officer about one weak point in a file such as a large deposit, a gap, a past refusal or travel history, tied to the evidence attached.","category":"immigration","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["build"],"role":["individual","student","job-seeker"],"subject":["law"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","document"],"output":["docs","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["legal"],"invocation":"user","effort":"standard","interaction":"one-shot","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","tags":["letter-of-explanation","visa-cover-note","unexplained-deposit","employment-gap","previous-refusal"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["prepare-financial-evidence-for-visa","write-visa-application-cover-letter","respond-to-visa-refusal"]},"args":[{"name":"issue","description":"The point an officer might question (for example a 6,000 deposit last month, a 2-year gap in study, a previous refusal, a change of sponsor), with the true explanation in your words.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"application","description":"The country and visa or permit, and the requirement this issue relates to.","type":"string","required":true},{"name":"evidence","description":"The documents you have that support the explanation. Optional but important.","type":"text"}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["What the officer is likely to ask","Letter of explanation","Evidence index","Gaps"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nOfficers decide on the papers, often in minutes, and they refuse when something looks unexplained rather than wrong. A letter of explanation pre-empts the question an officer will ask on reading the file: where did this money come from, what happened in this gap, why was the last application refused and what has changed. It works when it is short, specific, consistent with every other document, and backed by evidence that is attached and indexed. It fails when it is long and emotional, introduces facts not in the forms, contradicts an earlier application, or makes claims without proof. A letter cannot change a requirement; it only explains facts.\n\nApplication: {{application}}\n\n<issue>\n{{issue}}\n</issue>\n{{#evidence}}\n<evidence>\n{{evidence}}\n</evidence>\n{{/evidence}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. What the officer is likely to ask about this issue, and which requirement it touches.\n2. Draft the letter, under one page: a reference line with placeholders, one sentence on what the letter explains, the facts in date order, the explanation, each statement tied to an attached document by exhibit number, and a closing that offers more information. Plain, polite, first person.\n3. Evidence index: each document, what it proves, and its exhibit number.\n4. Gaps: any statement in the letter that is not yet backed by a document, and what would prove it; and any point where the explanation may not fully answer the requirement, to raise with a regulated adviser.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- Use only the facts given and say only what is true. Never add facts, soften a refusal history or contradict an earlier application.\n- Keep it short and unemotional; no pleading, flattery or pressure.\n- Do not promise that the letter will resolve the issue.\n- Mark placeholders [LIKE THIS] for reference numbers and dates not given; keep ID numbers out.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What the officer is likely to ask\n## Letter of explanation\n## Evidence index\nTable: Exhibit | Document | What it proves.\n## Gaps\nBullets.\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
